WEBVTT

00:00.900 --> 00:03.730
Graph the line that represents
a proportional relationship

00:03.730 --> 00:07.130
between y and x with
a unit rate 0.4.

00:07.130 --> 00:10.390
That is, a change
of one unit in x

00:10.390 --> 00:13.780
corresponds to a change
of 0.4 units in y.

00:13.780 --> 00:15.410
And they also ask
us to figure out

00:15.410 --> 00:18.100
what the equation of
this line actually is.

00:18.100 --> 00:20.650
So let me get my scratch pad
out and we could think about it.

00:20.650 --> 00:24.350
So let's just think about some
potential x and y values here.

00:24.350 --> 00:27.077
So let's think about some
potential x and y values.

00:27.077 --> 00:29.410
So when we're thinking about
proportional relationships,

00:29.410 --> 00:34.750
that means that y is going to be
equal to some constant times x.

00:34.750 --> 00:36.500
So if we have a
proportional relationship,

00:36.500 --> 00:39.570
if you have zero x's, it doesn't
matter what your constant here

00:39.570 --> 00:41.670
is, you're going
to have zero y's.

00:41.670 --> 00:44.740
So the point 0, 0
should be on your line.

00:44.740 --> 00:48.280
So if this is the point 0, 0,
this should be on my line right

00:48.280 --> 00:49.340
over there.

00:49.340 --> 00:52.330
Now, let's think about what
happens as we increase x.

00:52.330 --> 00:55.620
So if x goes from 0
to 1, we already know

00:55.620 --> 00:57.560
that a change of 1
unit in x corresponds

00:57.560 --> 01:00.610
to a change of 0.4 units in y.

01:00.610 --> 01:05.470
So if x increases by 1, then
y is going to increase by 0.4.

01:05.470 --> 01:08.932
It's not so easy to
graph this 1 comma 0.4.

01:08.932 --> 01:12.560
The 0.4 is hard to graph on this
little tool right over here.

01:12.560 --> 01:14.730
So let's try to get this
to be a whole number.

01:14.730 --> 01:16.850
So then when x
increases another 1,

01:16.850 --> 01:18.900
y is going to
increase by 0.4 again.

01:18.900 --> 01:20.930
It's going to get to 0.8.

01:20.930 --> 01:23.580
When x increases
again by 1, then y

01:23.580 --> 01:25.370
is going to increase
by 0.4 again.

01:25.370 --> 01:27.540
It's going to get to 1.2.

01:27.540 --> 01:32.010
If x increases again, y is
going to increase by 0.4 again.

01:32.010 --> 01:32.920
So just to 1.6.

01:32.920 --> 01:36.400
Notice, every time x
is increasing by 1,

01:36.400 --> 01:38.940
y is increasing by 0.4.

01:38.940 --> 01:40.950
That's exactly what
they told us here.

01:40.950 --> 01:43.630
Now, if x increases
by 1 again to 5,

01:43.630 --> 01:46.350
then y is going to
increase 0.4 to 2.

01:46.350 --> 01:49.840
And I like this point because
this is nice and easy to graph.

01:49.840 --> 01:55.130
So we see that the point 0,
0 and the point 5 comma 2

01:55.130 --> 01:56.357
should be on this graph.

01:56.357 --> 01:57.190
And I could draw it.

01:57.190 --> 02:00.060
And I'm going to do it on
the tool in a second as well.

02:00.060 --> 02:04.760
So it'll look
something like this.

02:04.760 --> 02:08.070
And notice the slope of
this actual graph over here.

02:08.070 --> 02:10.509
Notice the slope of
this actual graph.

02:10.509 --> 02:15.480
If our change in x is 5.

02:15.480 --> 02:17.810
So notice, here our
change in x is 5.

02:17.810 --> 02:19.285
Our change in x is 5.

02:19.285 --> 02:20.160
You see that as well.

02:20.160 --> 02:24.220
When you go from 0 to 5,
this change in x is 5.

02:24.220 --> 02:26.500
Change in x is equal to 5.

02:26.500 --> 02:29.340
What was our
corresponding change in y?

02:29.340 --> 02:31.780
Well, our corresponding change
in y when our change in x

02:31.780 --> 02:36.040
was 5, our change
in y was equal to 2.

02:36.040 --> 02:38.610
And you see that here,
when x went from 0 to 5,

02:38.610 --> 02:41.760
y went from 0 to 2.

02:41.760 --> 02:45.920
So our change in y in this
circumstance is equal to 2.

02:45.920 --> 02:48.890
So our slope, which
is change in y

02:48.890 --> 02:53.320
over change in x, is
the rate of change

02:53.320 --> 02:54.960
of your vertical
axis with respect

02:54.960 --> 03:02.230
to your horizontal axis, is
going to be equal to 2 over 5,

03:02.230 --> 03:03.450
or 2/5.

03:03.450 --> 03:07.460
Which if you wrote it as
a decimal is equal to 0.4.

03:07.460 --> 03:10.807
So this right over
here is your slope.

03:10.807 --> 03:12.390
So I'm going to do
this with the tool.

03:12.390 --> 03:14.140
But first, let's
also think about what

03:14.140 --> 03:17.560
the equation of this
line is going to be.

03:17.560 --> 03:21.460
Well, we know that y is equal
to some constant times x.

03:21.460 --> 03:23.700
And we know that
the point 5, 2 is

03:23.700 --> 03:25.780
on this line right over here.

03:25.780 --> 03:30.050
So we could say, well, when x
is equal to 5, y is equal to 2.

03:30.050 --> 03:35.960
Or, when y equals 2, we
have k times 5, or k is

03:35.960 --> 03:42.660
equal to-- dividing both sides
by 5, you can't see that.

03:42.660 --> 03:49.410
If I divide both sides by 5,
I'm left with k is equal to 2/5.

03:49.410 --> 03:50.187
Which makes sense.

03:50.187 --> 03:51.270
We're used to seeing this.

03:51.270 --> 03:54.130
When we have y is equal
to something times x,

03:54.130 --> 03:58.140
this something right over
here is going to be our slope.

03:58.140 --> 04:04.490
So the equation of the
line is y is equal to 0.4x.

04:04.490 --> 04:05.560
So let's fill this in.

04:05.560 --> 04:08.270
Let's actually do
the exercise now.

04:08.270 --> 04:11.490
So we had two points,
one was the point 0, 0.

04:11.490 --> 04:13.340
When x is 0, y is 0.

04:13.340 --> 04:18.339
And when x is 5, y
is 0.4 times that.

04:18.339 --> 04:20.490
So it's y is equal to 2.

04:20.490 --> 04:27.470
And we said the equation is
y is equal to 0.4 times x.

04:27.470 --> 04:29.930
So let's check our answer.
